1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,810
While the Book of Esther is often told as a royal love story, an orphan girl who wins the king's heart and becomes queen,

2
00:00:12,810 --> 00:00:17,110
it is actually one of the most suspenseful rescue stories in all of Scripture.

3
00:00:17,110 --> 00:00:23,820
And it is the only book in the Bible where the name of God never appears, yet His hand is on every page.

4
00:00:23,820 --> 00:00:32,060
When it comes to the grand story of salvation, Esther operates as a hidden hand, a royal mediator, and a great reversal.

5
00:00:32,060 --> 00:00:36,010
Here is the breakdown of Esther's significance to redemptive history.

6
00:00:36,010 --> 00:00:39,940
One. The Hidden Hand — the God who is never named.

7
00:00:39,940 --> 00:00:44,240
The Silence. No prayer is quoted, and no miracle is shown.

8
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:50,680
Instead we find a sleepless king, a forgotten record, and a petition made at exactly the right moment.

9
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:54,880
The Providence. Mordecai trusts that deliverance will come,

10
00:00:54,880 --> 00:01:00,960
and he asks Esther, "who knoweth whether thou art come to the kingdom for such a time as this?" (Esther 4:14)

11
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:08,510
The Salvation Link. The God who seemed silent in Persia was quietly guarding the people through whom the Messiah would come.

12
00:01:08,510 --> 00:01:13,690
As the apostle Paul writes, "all things work together for good to them that love God." (Romans 8:28)

13
00:01:13,690 --> 00:01:18,350
Two. The Ancient Enemy — the war against the promised Seed.

14
00:01:18,350 --> 00:01:27,910
The Agagite. Haman is called "the Agagite", widely understood as a descendant of Agag, king of Amalek, Israel's oldest enemy.

15
00:01:27,910 --> 00:01:33,510
The Decree. He plots to destroy every Jew in the empire in a single day.

16
00:01:33,510 --> 00:01:37,180
Had he succeeded, the line of promise would have been cut off.

17
00:01:37,180 --> 00:01:41,720
The Salvation Link. This is the long war first announced in Eden:

18
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:47,310
"I will put enmity between thee and the woman, and between thy seed and her seed." (Genesis 3:15)

19
00:01:47,310 --> 00:01:51,800
Esther shows that no decree of man can stop the Seed God has promised.

20
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:55,500
In the fulness of time, Christ was born from that very people.

21
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:59,170
Three. The Royal Mediator — a picture of Christ.

22
00:01:59,170 --> 00:02:02,960
The Risk. To approach the king uncalled meant death.

23
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:07,560
Esther fasts three days, then goes: "and if I perish, I perish." (Esther 4:16)

24
00:02:07,560 --> 00:02:12,330
The Sceptre. "The king held out to Esther the golden sceptre that was in his hand. (Esther 5:2)

25
00:02:12,330 --> 00:02:16,240
So Esther drew near, and touched the top of the sceptre." (Esther 5:2)

26
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:21,490
The True and Better Esther. Esther risked her life for her people; Jesus gave His.

27
00:02:21,490 --> 00:02:24,970
Esther was received by grace, and through Christ we are invited:

28
00:02:24,970 --> 00:02:32,260
"Let us therefore come boldly unto the throne of grace, that we may obtain mercy, and find grace to help in time of need." (Hebrews 4:16)

29
00:02:32,260 --> 00:02:36,070
Four. The Great Reversal — from sorrow to joy.

30
00:02:36,070 --> 00:02:40,710
The Gallows. "So they hanged Haman on the gallows that he had prepared for Mordecai." (Esther 7:10)

31
00:02:40,710 --> 00:02:44,850
The Unchangeable Law. The king's first decree could not be undone,

32
00:02:44,850 --> 00:02:49,870
so a second was written and sealed with the king's ring, a writing that "may no man reverse." (Esther 8:8)

33
00:02:49,870 --> 00:02:56,250
The Gospel Turn. The day meant for destruction became the month "turned unto them from sorrow to joy." (Esther 9:22)

34
00:02:56,250 --> 00:03:01,260
At the cross, the very weapon the enemy raised against the Son became the enemy's own defeat.

35
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:05,760
Christ "made a shew of them openly, triumphing over them in it." (Colossians 2:15)

36
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:11,290
The sentence against us was real, but God wrote a better word, sealed in the blood of His Son.

37
00:03:11,290 --> 00:03:16,000
Esther reminds us that even when God seems silent, He is never absent.

38
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,540
He raised up a queen for such a time as this, guarded the line of the promised Seed,

39
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:23,290
and turned a day of death into a feast of joy.

40
00:03:23,290 --> 00:03:28,890
And in Jesus, the true and better Mediator, the King's sceptre is held out to all who will come.

41
00:03:28,890 --> 00:03:34,050
The hidden hand of God in Esther points us to the open, nail-scarred hands of Christ.
